WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.810
I want to talk about an interesting issue
with everybody today.

00:00:02.810 --> 00:00:04.009
Should Democrats

00:00:04.009 --> 00:00:07.279
have Republicans in their administration.

00:00:07.279 --> 00:00:11.720
Today I'm going to tell you about what two
democratic candidates said about this tool.

00:00:11.720 --> 00:00:16.220
See Gabbert and Joe Biden and then I'm going
to talk about the concept of Republicans and

00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:22.460
democratic administrations, whether there's
virtue in in some abstract sense in bipartisanship.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:24.120
And lastly, I'll tell you why.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:28.270
This is actually a pretty meaningless promise
to even be making, so let's get right into

00:00:28.270 --> 00:00:29.270
it.

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:31.380
You all know I am not a supporter of tool.

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:38.039
See Gabard, she is a soon to be former Congresswoman
from Hawaii who has missed the majority of

00:00:38.039 --> 00:00:39.660
her house votes recently.

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:44.630
Uh, I am a, uh, supporting other people in
the democratic primary, not her, but she was

00:00:44.630 --> 00:00:49.559
asked would you have Republicans and independence
in your hypothetical administration?

00:00:49.559 --> 00:00:53.469
And she initially said, not only is she open
to it, she would commit to it.

00:00:53.469 --> 00:00:54.550
Let's check it out.

00:00:54.550 --> 00:01:00.760
You talked so much about reaching across the
aisle.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:09.030
Yes, you can commit to Republicans or independent
into a gabardine administration.

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:10.780
I would make that commitment.

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:16.900
The first and foremost a qualification for
those that I would select for a service in

00:01:16.900 --> 00:01:21.640
my cabinet and in my administration would
be those who are committed to putting service

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:24.750
to the our country and the American people
first.

00:01:24.750 --> 00:01:31.190
We should not be afraid of diversity of ideas
and, and take inspiration from the team of

00:01:31.190 --> 00:01:36.810
rivals that Abraham Lincoln to cha, uh, chose
to surround himself with, um, and, and draw

00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:41.800
strength from that and being able to deliver
that service and results to the American people

00:01:41.800 --> 00:01:43.370
rising above partisan interests.

00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:47.790
And again, putting service to the people above
everything else.

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:51.850
She is willing to make a commitment to having
Republicans in her administration.

00:01:51.850 --> 00:01:56.990
Now, of course, this is always an easier commitment
to make when you have absolutely no shot at

00:01:56.990 --> 00:02:01.210
having an administration in which you would
decide about having Republicans or Democrats.

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:05.810
But let's take her at her word, except she
was asked to confirm.

00:02:05.810 --> 00:02:09.299
Oh, so you're committing to Republicans in
your administration.

00:02:09.299 --> 00:02:13.590
She immediately backtracks and says, well,
I'm committing not to require that everybody

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:16.310
be a Democrat, which is a very different thing
to commit to.

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:17.780
Then having Republicans.

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:20.099
This is the second part of the clip, which
a lot of people have not seen.

00:02:20.099 --> 00:02:25.999
So just to clarify that right now, if you're
president of the United States, there will

00:02:25.999 --> 00:02:34.930
be, I will commit to making sure that there
is no partisan, um, requirement for those

00:02:34.930 --> 00:02:37.370
who I choose to serve in my administration.

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:43.650
Instead, looking at that most important qualification,
are you someone who is committed to serving

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:48.430
the people of this country, uh, not serving
for your own personal profit or benefit or

00:02:48.430 --> 00:02:54.420
anything else and that you've got the experience
to be able to, um, to be able to do the job?

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:58.279
Okay, so before we even go further, notice
that she Insta flips.

00:02:58.279 --> 00:03:01.920
She first says, I, yes, Republicans, I would
commit to that.

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:06.189
And then she's asked, so are you, you're committing
to Republicans and your administration?

00:03:06.189 --> 00:03:10.780
And she says, well, I'm committing not to
require everybody be a Democrat.

00:03:10.780 --> 00:03:11.849
Okay, cool tool.

00:03:11.849 --> 00:03:12.849
So a good one.

00:03:12.849 --> 00:03:18.799
Now before we go further, remember that Joe
Biden said he would be open to a Republican

00:03:18.799 --> 00:03:22.870
running mate, but he can't think of one specifically
that he'd be interested in right now.

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:25.020
And he was crushed by some Democrats.

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:26.020
Remember this clip?

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:28.139
Why wonder if Joe Biden would consider

00:03:28.139 --> 00:03:30.879
choosing a Republican as a running mate?

00:03:30.879 --> 00:03:35.430
The answer is I would, but I can't think of
one now.

00:03:35.430 --> 00:03:38.739
No, no, no, no, no, no, no, no, no.

00:03:38.739 --> 00:03:40.200
Look, no, I'm serious.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:41.200
No.

00:03:41.200 --> 00:03:42.200
Here's what I mean.

00:03:42.200 --> 00:03:43.200
Let me find out.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:47.829
Know other, some really decent Republicans
that are out there still.

00:03:47.829 --> 00:03:55.150
Now let's think about this more theoretically
and then specifically, this is basically the

00:03:55.150 --> 00:03:57.060
right answer in principle, right?

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:02.700
I'm open to anyone who is the right person
regardless of party.

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:07.859
If you're asking me, are there any Republicans
today in 2020 that I can think of as running

00:04:07.859 --> 00:04:08.859
mates?

00:04:08.859 --> 00:04:13.430
I can't think of one that's actually the right
answer, but let's take it a step further.

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:16.720
There's nothing inherently good about bipartisan
bipartisanship.

00:04:16.720 --> 00:04:21.760
We wouldn't say, imagine that one party is
made up of all white supremacists.

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:26.270
We wouldn't say, well, I want people from
all parties in my administration, including

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:27.639
the white supremacist party.

00:04:27.639 --> 00:04:28.750
Nobody would expect that.

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:32.629
Now, I'm not saying the Republican party is
all made up of white supremacists, but the

00:04:32.629 --> 00:04:39.060
point I'm trying to make is there's not any
inherent virtue in saying we will have people

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:44.419
from all parties or from all political perspectives
in my administration, there are bad parties,

00:04:44.419 --> 00:04:46.460
there are bad political perspectives.

00:04:46.460 --> 00:04:51.150
So the first thing I think is important to
understand is that on the left we have to

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:52.460
be consistent.

00:04:52.460 --> 00:04:53.460
Okay?

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:58.240
If we like the idea of tool seeping open to
Republicans and the administration, it should

00:04:58.240 --> 00:04:59.690
be the same for Joe Biden.

00:04:59.690 --> 00:05:01.039
Joe is being upfront.

00:05:01.039 --> 00:05:03.780
He says, I'm open to it, but I can't think
of anybody.

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:04.780
I get it.

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:08.930
There's a difference between a Republican
vice president and a Republican somewhere

00:05:08.930 --> 00:05:10.370
else in an administration.

00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:15.330
But if we liked the idea of democratic candidates
being open to Republicans and administrations,

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:17.800
then we've got to be okay with it no matter
who says it.

00:05:17.800 --> 00:05:19.979
But I don't actually care about that.

00:05:19.979 --> 00:05:25.650
So specifically the idea of should Democrats
have Republicans in their administration?

00:05:25.650 --> 00:05:28.389
In theory, it's not controversial.

00:05:28.389 --> 00:05:35.320
We should have the group of people to advance
a progressive agenda in total.

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:41.370
If that includes strategically selecting some
Republicans to put in some particular positions,

00:05:41.370 --> 00:05:42.419
then why not?

00:05:42.419 --> 00:05:47.970
We should not ideologically rule out Republicans
with a partisan test.

00:05:47.970 --> 00:05:52.860
But the reality is that number one, for the
most part, it's not going to be Republicans

00:05:52.860 --> 00:05:54.740
that help us achieve a progressive agenda.

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:59.449
So again, there's no particular virtue in
saying, sure, I'll have Republicans, but let

00:05:59.449 --> 00:06:02.650
me kind of give you the cheat sheet real quick.

00:06:02.650 --> 00:06:06.160
This entire question is a meaningless political
game.

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:10.310
I'll explain to you why Tulsi didn't say cabinet.

00:06:10.310 --> 00:06:16.180
She said, administration, and then she said,
well, I'm not saying I would have Republicans.

00:06:16.180 --> 00:06:19.740
I'm saying I wouldn't require, everybody has
to be a Democrat.

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:24.099
Joe Biden said, sure, I'd have a Republican
vice president except I can't actually think

00:06:24.099 --> 00:06:25.099
of one.

00:06:25.099 --> 00:06:30.659
The number of people in presidential administrations
is gigantic and it's actually no big deal

00:06:30.659 --> 00:06:35.700
to put Republicans in some positions, including
the less overtly political ones, and I would

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:37.289
go even further.

00:06:37.289 --> 00:06:42.639
Could I find a tolerable Republican to put
in my administration in a position of some

00:06:42.639 --> 00:06:49.190
actual power to appease those who find virtue
abstractly in bipartisanship?

00:06:49.190 --> 00:06:50.190
Sure.

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:53.580
If you said to me, you've got to find some
prominent Republican and put, put him or her

00:06:53.580 --> 00:06:58.400
in your administration, I could find a role
for like Utah Senator and former Massachusetts

00:06:58.400 --> 00:06:59.500
governor, Mitt Romney.

00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:03.870
Based on the things he did correctly in Massachusetts,
I could find something for him to do where

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:08.270
he wouldn't really do any damage and I could
say, Hey, I've got Republicans in my administration.

00:07:08.270 --> 00:07:11.670
Would he be the best person for whatever position?

00:07:11.670 --> 00:07:13.180
Maybe, maybe not.

00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:18.090
But if the point is to find Republicans to
say, you have Republicans in your administration,

00:07:18.090 --> 00:07:20.860
okay, I can find something for them to do.

00:07:20.860 --> 00:07:26.400
The point here is, and the takeaway is this
is a meaningless political game in that it

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:31.200
all comes down to what you mean by Republicans
in your administration.

00:07:31.200 --> 00:07:35.639
I'm fine with what tools he said, even though
she walked it back and it doesn't even really

00:07:35.639 --> 00:07:37.210
sound like she meant it.

00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:43.681
I'm fine with what Joe Biden said as long
as he and I agree that in the 2020 Republican

00:07:43.681 --> 00:07:50.350
party, I can't think of any prominent Republican
that would be better as a vice president than

00:07:50.350 --> 00:07:53.180
someone that we can actually find choosing
from Democrats.

00:07:53.180 --> 00:07:55.930
So let's not go nuts with the litmus tests.

00:07:55.930 --> 00:08:01.590
Let's not go nuts with the concern trolling
the gasping that Joe Biden is open to a Republican

00:08:01.590 --> 00:08:05.610
vice president, even though he can't actually
think of one that Tulsi Gabbert said she would

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:08.410
be open to Republicans or her administration.

00:08:08.410 --> 00:08:12.460
Biden would obviously pick a Democrat for
vice president if he's the nominee.

00:08:12.460 --> 00:08:14.020
Tool C has no shot.

00:08:14.020 --> 00:08:18.289
It's an interesting theoretical discussion
and that's that there's no particular virtue

00:08:18.289 --> 00:08:23.349
in bipartisanship, but if you're trying to
check boxes that you have people from all

00:08:23.349 --> 00:08:31.110
parties, you can do it safely and without
actually putting toxic Republicans in particular

00:08:31.110 --> 00:08:35.440
in positions where they would have any serious
amount of power or influence over the policy

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:36.440
of your administration.

00:08:36.440 --> 00:08:36.444
That's all this is.

